## Founding & History
Stargazer Wine was established in 2012, with its first vintage released that year. The winery was founded by Samantha (Sam) Connew, a New Zealander who was inspired by Tasmanian Riesling and Pinot Noir. After gaining extensive international winemaking experience, Connew decided to establish her own winery in Tasmania. In 2016, she purchased an 11-hectare site in the Coal River Valley, which included one hectare already planted with Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. The name "Stargazer" pays tribute to Abel Tasman, the Dutch explorer and navigator who was the first European to sight Tasmania, reflecting his time spent gazing at the heavens. This name also links Connew's New Zealand origins to her adopted home of Tasmania. A significant milestone for the winery was the opening of its dedicated tasting room at the Palisander Vineyard in late October 2025.

## People
Samantha (Sam) Connew is the owner, principal winemaker, sales and marketing manager, and vineyard hand at Stargazer Wine. Connew's journey into wine began after studying law and arts in Christchurch, New Zealand, where part-time work at a wine bar ignited her passion. She gained experience through vintages in Oregon, Italy, Spain, and various regions across Australia. Notably, she spent a decade as the Senior Winemaker at Wirra Wirra Vineyards in McLaren Vale, followed by roles at Tower Estate in the Hunter Valley and the Australian Wine Research Institute. Connew is an influential figure in the Australian wine industry, having served as the youngest judge and first woman to chair the Sydney Royal Wine Show, and currently chairs the Royal Adelaide Wine Show. She has also contributed to the advisory board of the Australian Women in Wine Awards and as a board member of Wine Tasmania, and tutors at the Len Evans Tutorial. Bryn Williams collaborates with Connew, overseeing the viticulture at Stargazer.

## Vineyard & Site
Stargazer Wine's primary vineyard, known as Palisander, is located in the Tea Tree sub-region on the northwestern side of the Coal River Valley in Tasmania. The original plantings at Palisander date back to 2002 and 2004. Connew purchased the 11-hectare site in 2016, which initially had one hectare planted with Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. The vineyard was expanded in late 2017 with an additional two hectares of Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, and Riesling, and further plantings occurred in 2021. Currently, the estate-grown Palisander vineyard spans five hectares.

The soil type at Palisander is predominantly brown dermosol on Jurassic dolerite, characterized as clay loam over ironstone with a high calcium content. This soil structure is known to promote thick grape skins, contributing to enhanced flavor. Varietals planted include Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling, Gamay, Gewürztraminer, Pinot Gris, and Pinot Meunier. The Gamay was planted in 2021, featuring BGY19 and 222 clones. Shiraz, used in the 'Kura' blend, was planted in 2004.

Stargazer employs sustainable farming practices, treating the vineyard holistically as a farm rather than a monoculture. This approach includes using sheep for weed control, free-ranging chickens, and bees for pollination. Over 1000 native species have been planted to enhance biodiversity, and the property also features an olive grove, an orchard of nut and fruit trees, and berries. A worm farm is utilized to produce "worm tea" for vineyard application. Samantha Connew's philosophy emphasizes leaving the land in a better condition than she found it.

## Cellar Door Experience
Stargazer Wine's tasting room is located at 37 Rosewood Lane, Tea Tree, in the Coal River Valley, offering views of the Chardonnay and Pinot Noir vines at the Palisander vineyard. Visiting the tasting room is primarily by appointment, though walk-ins are welcome subject to capacity. Booking times are available Friday through Monday at 11:00 a.m., 1:30 p.m., and 3:30 p.m.. Another source indicates booking times at 10:00 a.m., 12:30 p.m., and 3:00 p.m. on these days.

The tasting format is a guided, tutored session, conducted around a bespoke communal table with a limited number of guests (up to 12) to ensure an intimate and personal experience. The 90-minute tasting includes a selection of current release wines and occasional special treats, costing $35 per person. Wine Club members receive complimentary tastings. For the foreseeable future, these sessions are personally hosted by winemaker Samantha Connew, allowing guests to engage directly with her winemaking philosophy and expertise. The glass-walled tasting room provides an intimate setting within the vineyard, and guests can also access a porch overlooking the valley. The tasting room is accessible, with prior notification recommended for specific needs.

## Food & Hospitality
No reliable information found from public sources regarding a dedicated restaurant or kitchen at Stargazer Wine. The tasting room does not accommodate large tour groups or private functions. However, Wine Club members may have access to "tasting room lunches" as a benefit.

## Awards & Recognition
Samantha Connew was named the 2025 Australian Society of Viticulture and Oenology Winemaker of the Year. She holds a distinguished position in the Australian wine industry, having been the youngest judge and the first woman to serve as Chair of Judges at the Sydney Royal Wine Show. She is also the current Chair of Judges at the Royal Adelaide Wine Show. Stargazer has been recognized as "one of the most exciting prospects in Tasmania".

## Sustainability & Certifications
Stargazer Wine is committed to sustainable farming practices. The Palisander vineyard employs a holistic approach, integrating sheep for weed control, free-ranging chickens, and bees for pollination. Over 1000 native species have been planted to enhance biodiversity. The property also includes an olive grove, an orchard of nut and fruit trees, and berries, treating the vineyard as a diverse farm ecosystem rather than a monoculture. A worm farm is utilized to produce "worm tea" for vineyard use. Samantha Connew's philosophy is centered on improving the land for future generations. No specific organic or biodynamic certifications were found in the public sources.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Stargazer Wine's tasting room is 37 Rosewood Lane, Tea Tree, Tasmania, 7017. It is located approximately 30 minutes' drive from Hobart, or 20 minutes northeast of Hobart. The phone number is 0473 668 494 or +61 473 668 494. The official website is stargazerwine.com.au. For inquiries, the email address is info@stargazerwine.com.au. Stargazer maintains a presence on social media platforms including Facebook and Instagram.

For visitors, Stargazer offers shipping services; wine purchases can be sent directly to a home address (minimum 6 bottles, $15 flat rate freight within Australia). The winery also participates in the Tasmanian Mixed Dozen freight scheme and can arrange quotes for international shipping. The tasting room is accessible, and it is recommended to notify the venue in advance for any specific access needs.

## Notable / Distinctive
Stargazer Wine is genuinely distinctive due to the singular vision and extensive experience of its owner and winemaker, Samantha Connew. Her background, including significant leadership roles in the Australian wine industry such as chairing major wine shows and serving on industry boards, sets her apart. The winery's name, "Stargazer," is a direct tribute to explorer Abel Tasman, symbolically linking Connew's New Zealand heritage with her passion for Tasmania.

A core aspect of Stargazer's philosophy is its commitment to cool-climate viticulture in Tasmania and a winemaking approach that celebrates the unique variations and "quirks" of each vintage, rather than aiming for consistency. The newly opened tasting room offers an intimate, guided experience, often personally hosted by Samantha Connew, providing a direct connection between visitors and the winemaker. Furthermore, the holistic and sustainable farming practices at the Palisander vineyard, incorporating sheep, chickens, bees, native plantings, and an olive grove, contribute to a diverse and healthy ecosystem. The production of unique blends like 'Tupelo' (Pinot Gris/Pinot Blanc/Gewurztraminer) and 'Rada' (Pinot Noir/Gamay), along with the use of ceramic concrete eggs for some Riesling fermentation, further highlight the winery's distinctive approach.

